          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My father had a heart attack at 55. What does that mean for my own risk, and how does guidance use family history when deciding about a statin?",
          "answer": "**If you have chest pain or pressure, pain spreading to your arm, jaw or back, shortness of breath, or sweating or nausea with those symptoms, call emergency services rather than waiting.**\n\nYour father's heart attack at 55 is a family history fact that can matter for your heart health. It does not mean you will have a heart attack. Guidance treats it as one factor among several. Heart disease risk is estimated mainly from age, sex, cholesterol, blood pressure, diabetes and smoking status, combined into a 10-year risk estimate (Lipids guidance, \"Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation\"). Family history of premature cardiovascular disease is listed as a \"risk-enhancing feature.\" It does not replace that estimate. It can help tip a decision when your estimated risk is borderline or intermediate, toward or away from starting a statin. The same document says a coronary artery calcium score may help when the decision is still unclear. Whether a statin is right for you is a decision to make with a clinician, who can use your full numbers.\n\nThe guidance I could retrieve does not define the age that counts as \"premature,\" so I can't tell you whether your father's event meets that definition. It also does not say which relatives count, or how much family history improves risk prediction beyond standard scores. How much weight to give it is therefore unresolved on the evidence I could find. I did not find a disagreement between authorities specifically about family history.\n\nThings you can influence include smoking, high blood pressure, high LDL cholesterol, diabetes, obesity, physical inactivity, and diet (Coronary artery disease reference). Hypertension guidance (\"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\") lists reducing salt, eating more vegetables, fruit and whole grains, being active most days, keeping weight healthy, limiting alcohol, and not smoking. That document is about blood pressure, but the same habits support heart health generally. I could not retrieve the detailed lifestyle guidance for heart disease prevention, so that is a gap in this answer.\n\nA useful next step is to ask a clinician to check your cholesterol, blood pressure and blood sugar, and to estimate your 10-year risk with a validated calculator. Tell them how old your father was when he had the heart attack, and whether any other close relatives had heart disease early. This is general information, not a diagnosis.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
